  by: agent  # 40cf
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 40cf
  clock: 8  # 40cf
  nonce: 40cf  # 40cf
  set:  # 40cf
    description: "Per ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_04072026_1518.md 'Elaboration on item 6' — the user explicitly identified this as the clearest remedy because it is the only proposal that changes the agent's incentive structure rather than adding tools/knowledge.\n\n**Change:** in packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/stop_hook.py:generate_guidance, branch when items_with_unread_blocking + unread_non_blocking > 0 and emit a *different* guidance file rather than the standard TODO/bakeoff one with an unread-message section appended. The new guidance:\n\n> REPLY-FIRST CYCLE — N unread human message(s)\n>\n> Take a breather from the \"forward-march vibe\" of autonomous mode. Do not start new code tasks, do not run bakeoffs, do not pick from tracker ready. Your only assignment is to clear reply debt. For each item:\n> 1. tracker show <ID> — load the full thread + description\n> 2. Classify: approval, directive, or question\n> 3. Questions: plan the investigation (Read/Grep, Explore subagent, WebSearch on allowlisted domains) BEFORE drafting\n> 4. tracker discuss <ID> — post the reply\n\n**Critical:** while reply debt exists, the hook does NOT emit 'AUTONOMOUS MODE: N TODOs block stopping'. The TODO list isn't shown at all. This is what gives the change teeth — the agent can't make progress on anything else without first clearing the reply.\n\n**Scope of suppression:** Default to treating all unread items as equally important, with the exception of unread items whose first characters match \\[\\[.+\\]\\] -- for example, if the user prefaces their remarks with [[IMPORTANT]] or [[do-right-fucking-now]] or [[Mclovin]]. This special treatment should not apply if the double-bracketed text contains spaces or if the double-bracketed text is anywhere other than the absolute first characters of the unread item. To avoid overcomplicating the logic of message triage, the presence of a [[]] annotation should only ever trigger a single distinct codepath. In other words, if the user annotation is ambiguous (e.g. \"[[Mclovin]]\"), it will still activate the alternate path, but that path is always going to encounter an LLM-mediated gate to the effect of \"There are a total of 3 unread messages from the human. Does the human-provided preface tag '[[Mclovin]]' imply that this message takes precedence over the other unreads?\"\n\n**Failure mode to design against:** the agent might learn that REPLY-FIRST CYCLE clears as soon as tracker discuss posts anything. The companion item ('--ack-thread enforcement') prevents this; without it, this change might just produce shorter drive-by replies.\n\n**Implementation cost:** a single-function behavior change in generate_guidance plus a preface detector and an LLM-gate call. Roughly:\n- ~40-60 lines for the default REPLY-FIRST CYCLE branch (swallow the AUTONOMOUS MODE TODO section, emit the \"forward-march vibe\" reminder, list unreads).\n- ~30-50 lines for the \\[\\[...\\]\\] preface detector (anchored at character 0, no internal whitespace, single codepath regardless of whether the tag is recognisable English — the gate decides).\n- ~40-60 lines to invoke the LLM-mediated precedence gate and thread its answer back into which unread the agent is steered toward first.\n- Tests: (a) the AUTONOMOUS MODE TODO section is omitted whenever any unread human message is present; (b) preface detection accepts `[[IMPORTANT]]`, `[[do-right-fucking-now]]`, `[[Mclovin]]` at position 0 and rejects `[[has space]]`, `leading text [[IMPORTANT]]`, and `[[ space before close]]`; (c) an ambiguous preface still enters the alternate path and the LLM gate receives a well-formed prompt naming every other unread; (d) when the gate declines to elevate, guidance falls back to the default equal-priority listing.\n\n**Validation plan** (per notebook): run for a week and measure (a) reply length per by:human-prompted discuss, (b) tool calls per reply turn (Read/Grep/Explore/WebSearch), (c) replies citing specific code locations / ADRs / PRs, (d) whether the agent ever uses WebSearch for a tracker reply (currently 0 across ~6500 records), (e) frequency of \\[\\[...\\]\\] prefaces in human discuss entries and how often the LLM gate elevates them, (f) whether an elevated item actually gets answered before the other unreads in the same session (the behavioural point of the alternate path).\n\nNOT a governance file — packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/stop_hook.py is application code."  # 40cf
